 PyJWK):\n        algorithm_ = key.algorithm_name\n    else:\n        algorithm_ = \"HS256\"\nelse:\n    algorithm_ = algorithm\n```\n\nSo `encode(payload, key=None, algorithm=None)` → algorithm_ = \"HS256\". That would fail (None key can't be HMAC). That's why the tests were changed to use `algorithm=\"none\"`.\n\nHmm wait, but the changelog entry from 2.10.0 says:\n\n```\n- Encoding and decoding payloads using the `none` algorithm by @jpadilla in `#c2629f6...`\n\n  Before:\n\n  .. code-block:: pycon\n\n   >>> import jwt\n   >>> jwt.encode({\"payload\": \"abc\"}, key=None, algorithm=None)\n\n  After:\n\n  .. code-block:: pycon\n\n   >>> import jwt\n   >>> jwt.encode({\"payload\": \"abc\"}, key=None, algorithm=\"none\")\n```\n\nSo in 2.10.0, `algorithm=None` no longer means \"none\"; you must pass `algorithm=\"none\"` explicitly. This is a consequence of PR #979 changing the default from \"HS256\" to None.\n\nHmm, but wait — there's a subtlety. In the testbed's current code (line 118): `algorithm_: str = algorithm if algorithm is not None else \"none\"`. So currently passing `algorithm=None` → \"none\". After the change, `algorithm=None` → HS256 (or JWK's alg). The tests in the testbed that use `algorithm=None` with `key=None` will need updating, per the upstream PR test diff.\n\nWait, but does the user's request mention this \"none\" behavior? The user says:\n\n\"If I pass a traditional key (like a string or bytes), it should default to using HS256 unless I explicitly specify a different algorithm.\"\n\nSo yes, `algorithm=None` with a string/bytes key → HS256. And \"none\" must be passed explicitly. This matches upstream PR #979 exactly.\n\n3. `api_jwt.py` encode just updates the type annotation and default. The JWT encode passes through to `api_jws.encode`, so the algorithm resolution happens in JWS. Good.\n\n4. Tests: the PR also updates tests:\n   - Adds `test_encode_default_algorithm` in test_api_jws.py\n   - Adds `test_encode_with_jwk` in test_api_jws.py\n   -