{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1788473915408,"sessionID":"ses_f96a9ebceffeKmiM5H0EW3IjUw","part":{"id":"prt_0695a1691001Hh9SveSZZejUtD","messageID":"msg_0695a084e001tCoQaZYq5XlAHI","sessionID":"ses_f96a9ebceffeKmiM5H0EW3IjUw","type":"reasoning","text":"The failure is in my manual test script, not the library: I created the JWK from a private RSA key. `RSAAlgorithm.to_jwk(priv)` includes \"d\" so `PyJWK` produced a private key object (an `RSAPrivateKey`), but... wait, the error says `'cryptography.hazmat.bindings._rust.openssl.rsa.RSA' object has no attribute 'verify'`. Hmm, that's odd. Actually the issue is the decode side: `jwt.decode(token, jwk, algorithms=['RS256'])` — the JWK contains the private key material (d, p, q...), so `from_jwk` returns an RSAPrivateKey, and `RSAAlgorithm.verify` calls `key.verify(...)` on the private key. In newer cryptography versions, RSAPrivateKey doesn't have `.verify` (it's not a public key).\n\nThis is a pre-existing library behavior issue (verifying with a private-key JWK), not related to my change — encode worked fine ('JWT+JWK header: {'alg': 'RS256', 'typ': 'JWT'}'). The decode failure is because I passed a private-key JWK to decode. Upstream `_verify_signature` uses `key.key` directly when given a PyJWK, and `RSAAlgorithm.verify` expects a public key. That's a pre-existing upstream behavior — in upstream, decoding with a private JWK would also fail the same way. Not my concern here.\n\nLet me redo the manual test properly: use the public JWK for decode, or just use a public-key JWK. Also verify the rest of the checks (items 2-5) which didn't run due to the exception.","time":{"start":1788473906833,"end":1788473915393},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The failure is in my manual test script, not the library: I created the JWK from a private RSA key. `RSAAlgorithm.to_jwk(priv)` includes \"d\" so `PyJWK` produced a private key object (an `RSAPrivateKey`), but... wait, the error says `'cryptography.hazmat.bindings._rust.openssl.rsa.RSA' object has no attribute 'verify'`. Hmm, that's odd. Actually the issue is the decode side: `jwt.decode(token, jwk, algorithms=['RS256'])` — the JWK contains the private key material (d, p, q...), so `from_jwk` returns an RSAPrivateKey, and `RSAAlgorithm.verify` calls `key.verify(...)` on the private key. In newer cryptography versions, RSAPrivateKey doesn't have `.verify` (it's not a public key).\n\nThis is a pre-existing library behavior issue (verifying with a private-key JWK), not related to my change — encode worked fine ('JWT+JWK header: {'alg': 'RS256', 'typ': 'JWT'}'). The decode failure is because I passed a private-key JWK to decode. Upstream `_verify_signature` uses `key.key` directly when given a PyJWK, and `RSAAlgorithm.verify` expects a public key. That's a pre-existing upstream behavior — in upstream, decoding with a private JWK would also fail the same way. Not my concern here.\n\nLet me redo the manual test properly: use the public JWK for decode, or just use a public-key JWK. Also verify the rest of the checks (items 2-5) which didn't run due to the exception.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}}}}
